WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.860
I wonder what the best Japanese learning materials are...

00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:10.520
Intro Music

00:00:10.520 --> 00:00:19.340
Good evening, folks! Today I want to talk about studying Japanese and the most useful materials for learning Japanese.

00:00:19.340 --> 00:00:24.100
Obviously, I haven't used all of the different materials in the world for studying Japanese,

00:00:24.100 --> 00:00:30.340
but I have used some, and I'm familiar with others to some extent, so I'll tell you about what I think, and my experience.

00:00:30.340 --> 00:00:37.420
One of the good things about studying Japanese is that there is such a wide range of good materials.

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:41.220
There's just a lot out there to choose from, so you can just go to the bookstore and look through a bunch of books

00:00:41.220 --> 00:00:42.820
and see which ones appeal to you.

00:00:42.820 --> 00:00:48.800
It comes down to learning style and taste, to some extent, but I'll just get into it.

00:00:49.120 --> 00:00:53.140
The first series that I used was called "Japanese for Busy People."

00:00:53.140 --> 00:00:58.540
The title is always kind of funny. I don't know why it's "for busy people," why not "for business people."

00:00:58.540 --> 00:01:05.940
The content of this series is basically for business. It's for simple business situations,

00:01:05.940 --> 00:01:09.000
like you go on a business trip to Tokyo and you talk to

00:01:09.000 --> 00:01:14.340
Mr. Tanaka from Tokyo Electric, and that kind of thing.

00:01:14.340 --> 00:01:18.860
The situations are kind of business. I found that aspect of it a little boring,

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:22.160
because I was never working in business in Japan;

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:28.300
I came here as a teacher, so it might not be completely relevant to you, in terms of content,

00:01:28.300 --> 00:01:33.960
but it's a good series, and if you go through volumes I, II, and maybe III, you'll learn quite a bit.

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.420
It's quite well laid out.

00:01:37.080 --> 00:01:44.080
It has a short conversation in each chapter, then it introduces some vocabulary and some grammar from that conversation

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:45.920
and gives you some exercises to practice it.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:51.600
And it's quite good, useful, standard Japanese, and I think it's a good series.

00:01:51.700 --> 00:01:58.840
There's another popular series called "Minna no Nihongo," or "Japanese for Everyone."

00:01:58.840 --> 00:02:04.040
That one is quite good as well; I haven't used the whole thing, but I've looked through it,

00:02:04.100 --> 00:02:08.960
and it looks quite good, and a lot of people swear by it, so that's another one to think about;

00:02:08.960 --> 00:02:13.060
it's "Minna no Nihongo," or "Japanese for Everyone," as it's called.

00:02:13.620 --> 00:02:17.940
Another series I used was one that's only published in Vancouver

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:25.000
It's published at a Japanese language school in Vancouver; I think it's called "Nakagawa" Language Centre,

00:02:25.000 --> 00:02:27.320
and it's called "Ganbatte." That's the name of the series.

00:02:27.320 --> 00:02:30.260
And that one was really good, it was really fun, it was really motivating.

00:02:30.460 --> 00:02:34.380
I really liked that the best as a pure beginner's textbook.

00:02:34.660 --> 00:02:39.780
There are five volumes -- number one is really simple -- but it goes up to five,

00:02:39.780 --> 00:02:44.800
and if you finish up to volume five, that prepares you for level one of the Japanese Proficiency Test.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:50.920
But all the conversations and the characters and stuff were quite cute and quite motivating.

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:53.820
It got me interested in learning Japanese more.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.620
Let me double-check the name of that centre.

00:02:55.620 --> 00:02:59.300
No, not "Nakagawa," sorry, it's Ninagawa Japanese Language Centre.

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:04.360
So it's hard to get those books -- I don't know if you can find the Ninagawa Ganbatte series -- but

00:03:04.360 --> 00:03:09.920
if you can find it online -- I think you can order it through the website of ninagawa.com -- so

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:15.000
have a look. If you can get it, it's good for beginners, at least for lower levels.

00:03:15.000 --> 00:03:17.500
So, those were the beginning textbooks.

00:03:17.500 --> 00:03:23.760
At an intermediate level, I loved this book, this one: "An Integrated Approach to Intermediate Japanese,"

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:28.060
by Akira Miura and Naomi Hanaoka McGloin.

00:03:28.260 --> 00:03:33.320
This one was awesome. This made me really love studying Japanese.

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:39.060
The way it was laid out, the conversations were fun and interesting; the stories and texts were interesting;

00:03:39.060 --> 00:03:43.220
and it wan't focused on business. It was focused on college life,

00:03:43.220 --> 00:03:46.120
and I wasn't a student, but I had been a student,

00:03:46.120 --> 00:03:49.120
so I could relate to college life a little better than corporate life,

00:03:49.120 --> 00:03:54.440
so the vocab and the topics and just the whole theme of this book was more interesting to me.

00:03:54.540 --> 00:03:58.400
Some people don't seem to like it. I've read some of the reviews on Amazon,

00:03:58.400 --> 00:04:00.380
and some people say they find it hard to follow.

00:04:00.380 --> 00:04:04.280
It might depend on your learning style. I'm a very inductive learner,

00:04:04.280 --> 00:04:08.060
I like to look at texts and conversations and kind of figure out what's going on,

00:04:08.060 --> 00:04:12.200
and look at the pieces and "Aw, this piece must be the verb. This must be the whatever."

00:04:12.380 --> 00:04:17.560
I like to figure things out, and that works well with this book, because

00:04:17.700 --> 00:04:23.200
the beginning of each chapter will have two or three different conversations or texts,

00:04:23.200 --> 00:04:29.060
so we can look at this one. It's got one conversation, then it's got another one, then it's got another one

00:04:29.240 --> 00:04:35.880
and then after that, it's got a text, and after that it has the vocabulary, and after the vocabulary,

00:04:36.840 --> 00:04:43.840
then it has -- that's the kanji -- then after that, it has the grammar points, and then it has grammar exercises.

00:04:44.380 --> 00:04:51.380
So, the texts and the conversations are separate from all of the breakdown and the explanation and the exercises.

00:04:51.380 --> 00:04:56.800
Those come after the texts. So, for me, that's fine. I like to look at the texts and the conversations

00:04:56.800 --> 00:05:02.620
and then flip back to the explanations when I need it, and then I can figure out the other things on my own

00:05:02.620 --> 00:05:06.340
to whatever extent I can. Then I can just flip back if I need an explanation.

00:05:06.340 --> 00:05:10.160
Some people like to have the explanation right up front, in their face, from the beginning,

00:05:10.160 --> 00:05:15.420
they're more deductive learners, they like to have it explained to them before they read the text,

00:05:15.460 --> 00:05:17.180
or at the same time.

00:05:17.180 --> 00:05:21.600
So, if you are like that, you might not like this book as much, but have a look at it.

00:05:21.600 --> 00:05:24.400
I really liked it, and this was my favourite book ever.

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:31.600
So, another one at the intermediate level was this one, which I didn't like -- I didn't have as much fun with it --

00:05:31.600 --> 00:05:36.820
but it was useful. It taught me a lot. It's this one, "Chuukyuu Kara Manabu Nihongo."

00:05:36.820 --> 00:05:40.340
That means "Japanese from the Intermediate Level."

00:05:40.400 --> 00:05:45.420
That's the meaning of this title, but this one is completely in Japanese.

00:05:45.420 --> 00:05:49.240
This other one, "An Integrated Approach," the one I showed you before,

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:52.240
this has a lot of English in it, and it has translations and explanations.

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:57.420
This one is all in Japanese, so this one is a bit more intense

00:05:57.420 --> 00:06:01.060
of an experience because you have to dive right in, it's all Japanese, no explanations. It doesn't

00:06:01.060 --> 00:06:05.700
even have a title in English. When I looked this up on Amazon to check if it's available,

00:06:06.260 --> 00:06:12.020
I had to type in the ISBN number, because when I typed in the Japanese, it didn't show up,

00:06:12.020 --> 00:06:20.280
because it was amazon.com, in America, but if I looked up the ISBN, then this name, this title,

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:24.360
appeared in Roman characters in Amazon. It doesn't even have an English name. You have to look up --

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:28.680
maybe I'll put the ISBN on the screen, so you can look it up if you want

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:38.580
This one's good. It has great texts. The texts are interesting stories, and there's a lot of stories in this,

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:43.300
there are some explanations of proverbs and different expressions in Japanese.

00:06:43.300 --> 00:06:48.340
I found it really useful to just go through the stories and highlight vocabulary I didn't know,

00:06:48.360 --> 00:06:57.640
and then look at the vocabulary lists and the grammar exercises after the stories.

00:06:57.740 --> 00:07:02.380
I found it hard to do all of the exercises without a classroom teacher.

00:07:02.380 --> 00:07:07.300
I did this on my own at home. I've actually never taken a Japanese lesson in my life,

00:07:08.320 --> 00:07:14.480
I did it all through self-study. I found it a bit hard to do this all at home, self-study style,

00:07:14.480 --> 00:07:18.660
but I got through it, and it was a bit of hard work, but I learned a lot from it.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:23.300
So this one is good, it's good material, just if you have a teacher, it would be easier for you,

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:28.780
because they can help you out with the exercises when the explanation in Japanese is not quite clear.

00:07:28.800 --> 00:07:34.540
Alright, so that was intermediate level. At a more advanced level, I used this one,

00:07:34.760 --> 00:07:41.760
"Chūkyū Kara Jōkyū e no Nihongo," so that's "Japanese from the Intermediate Level to the Advanced Level,"

00:07:41.760 --> 00:07:47.320
that's what that title means, or as they say, "Progressing from Intermediate to Advanced."

00:07:47.320 --> 00:07:54.220
That's the way they translated it. So this one was pretty good, it was kind of a bridge between intermediate

00:07:54.220 --> 00:07:59.660
to advanced, I got about halfway through this book. And it was good, it was useful, I learned a lot from it,

00:07:59.660 --> 00:08:06.520
but I started using authentic materials instead, I started just using newspapers and texts from magazines,

00:08:06.940 --> 00:08:13.680
and just trying to work through those and learn vocabulary. But this is good, it's similar to the "Integrated

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:18.600
Approach to Intermediate Japanese" one. It has texts that are interesting, and then it breaks it down with

00:08:18.740 --> 00:08:25.860
the vocab and the grammar exercises afterwards. I found it quite similar in format, and it was interesting to use,

00:08:25.860 --> 00:08:30.380
but at that level, I just thought it's better to get into the authentic materials.

00:08:30.380 --> 00:08:35.460
Okay, so those are the ones that I have used and I think are pretty good and I recommend.

00:08:35.460 --> 00:08:41.120
I've used some others here and there, and I used some different ones for kanji, which I'll probably talk about

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:46.440
in a different video, because kanji is a different beast entirely and deserves its own attention, but

00:08:46.440 --> 00:08:53.340
I recommend all those, especially this one, which was my favourite, "An Integrated Approach to Intermediate

00:08:53.340 --> 00:08:58.580
Japanese," but if you're a beginner, then you could get "Minna no Nihongo" or you could get "Japanese for

00:08:58.580 --> 00:09:04.860
Busy People," and work through it. One more note on "Japanese for Busy People," get the kana version.

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:11.300
Don't get the romanized version, there are two. "Romanized" means it has Roman characters in it.

00:09:11.300 --> 00:09:17.640
I think it has the Japanese as well, but I don't recommend using the romanized version because you

00:09:17.640 --> 00:09:23.660
might use the Roman letters as a crutch. It's good to get used to the kana letters right away from the beginning

00:09:23.660 --> 00:09:29.220
just to dive right in. You can learn them really quickly if you dive in and make yourself do it.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:34.560
So, make yourself do it, alright? Hope that's helpful. Have a nice night, guys! See ya.

00:09:34.580 --> 00:09:40.080
Outro Music

